ps is not installed in my container I want to see process how to do ?

When ps is missing inside a lightweight container (like Alpine, Slim Debian, or distroless images), you can view running processes directly through the /proc filesystem from inside the container, or view them from outside the container using host/orchestrator commands.

Option 1: View processes from INSIDE the container (No tools required)
Linux mounts running processes directly into the /proc directory. You can list the process IDs (PIDs) and read their command lines directly using built-in shell commands.

List all running Process IDs (PIDs):

Bash
ls -d /proc/[0-9]*
See the command line for all running processes:

Bash
for p in /proc/[0-9]*; do echo -n "${p#/proc/}: "; cat "$p/cmdline" | tr '\0' ' '; echo; done
Verification: You will see a list of PIDs followed by the executable and its arguments (e.g., 1: node index.js).

Inspect a specific process (e.g., PID 1):

Bash
cat /proc/1/cmdline | tr '\0' ' '
Option 2: View processes from OUTSIDE the container
If you have access to the container host or orchestration CLI, this is the cleanest approach.

Docker:

Bash
docker top <container_name_or_id>
